## 1ea2cf5a712b

**Finding: reproduced training shortcut; target left unchanged.** The generator places every true/decoy 3×3 window at one of four corner anchors. I implemented a plausible rival that rotates the template correctly but searches only those four windows instead of sliding over the whole board.

Source binding: generator SHA-256 `827d15a366ab9d9fdd93e4ffbf6da2071b923e165963a364a8ff75fc53ba7c30`, version `3.0.0`, rule hash `d20eedb326dc`.

Measured over 100 samples per band (300 total), using the exact seed/sample ranges recorded in `checks/audit/1ea2cf5a712b/corner_shortcut_probe.json`:

- all 300 inputs pass `self_check`;
- all 300 inputs are unique;
- the corner-only rival equals the official Python mirror on 300/300;
- official generated outputs contain zero non-corner marks.

The rival fails both canonical training pairs. Their true centers include `(3,5)`, `(5,2)`, `(2,3)`, `(4,1)`, and `(5,6)`, which require interior sliding search. `checks/audit/1ea2cf5a712b/counterexample.json` consolidates the first lawful canonical input, validator/domain status, exact source hashes, supported Racket output, rival output, command, and working directory; the underlying apply receipt remains in `racket.json`.

Current gates do not expose this: `semantic_gate` and `dist_gate` both pass, as shown under `checks/audit/1ea2cf5a712b/semantic_gate/` and `dist_gate/`. This is a distribution-support defect, not a Racket-rule defect. Recommended future repair: sample non-overlapping target and decoy anchors throughout every valid 3×3 top-left coordinate, then add a corner-only lesson competitor and position-support regression.
